<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 2.5 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-trigonometry-iii" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Trigonometry III</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In Grade <m>11</m> you extended the trigonometric ratios to angles of any size and used them to solve triangles. This chapter looks at how sine, cosine and tangent are related to each other and how they behave as the angle changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will derive the identity <m>\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1</m> from the trigonometric ratios. You will draw graphs of trigonometric functions in degrees and read off the amplitude, period, wavelength and phase angle of a wave. You will then use the identity to solve trigonometric equations, and transform waves using stretches and translations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Waves describe things that repeat: the tides at Mombasa, sound, radio and mobile phone signals, and the alternating current that powers your home.
    </p>
  </introduction>

